/**

\page memclave-reprod Reproduction Guide for Memclave

In this guide we provide instructions on reproducing results from our paper. For this guide we assume,
that you have already read and completed the setup instructions.

## MRAM Benchmark

The results of this benchmark are reported in Table 4 of our paper. The benchmark relies only
on code part of the client library and its examples. It can be run on UPMEM and on Memclave,
which one is selected using a CMake option. To build the Memclave version, run the following
chain of commands in the guest VM:
```bash
cmake -DENABLE_UPMEM_BENCHMARK=OFF -B build-mram-memclave .
cmake --build build-mram-memclave --target mram
```
This should yield an executable `build-mram-memclave/mram`. This executable outputs measurements
formatted as CSV on the command line. Capture the output like this:
```bash
./build-mram-memclave/mram > mram.csv
```

Building the UPMEM version is quite similar, you run almost the same commands, but on a normal
UPMEM machine and not the VM:
```bash
cmake -DENABLE_UPMEM_BENCHMARK=ON -b build-mram-upmem .
cmake --build build-mram-upmem --target mram
```
Execution works the same way. Simply capture the output into a file:
```bash
./build-mram-upmem/mram > mram-upmem.csv
```

## Crypto Benchmark

We present the performance of sealed EM transfer in table 3 of our paper. This measurement is based
on a benchmark of ChaCha20's performance on PIM. The benchmark consists of two components, a subkernel
and a guest side program.

The guest side program is part of the client library examples. It can be build just like all other
examples in the client library, by running the following commands inside the guest VM and `ime-client-library`
folder:
```bash
cmake -B build .
cmake --build build --target crypto-bench
```

The build process for the crypto benchmark is similarly straight-forward: Assuming you're currently cd'd
into the `ime` folder, running the following chain of commands will build the ChaCha20 benchmark subkernel:
```bash
docker run -it --rm -v ./:/ime memclave:latest bash
cd /ime
cmake -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=/usr/share/upmem/cmake/dpu.cmake -B build
cmake --build build --target chacha-bench
```
Now simply move the `chacha-bench` subkernel to the guest VM and execute the benchmark. Results are written
into a CSV file passed as a parameter to the executable.

## PrIM Benchmarks

We have ported 16 benchmarks of the PrIM project to Memclave. The subkernel parts of the ported benchmarks
are part of the `ime` folder, the guest parts are part of the `ime-client-library` folder. Both subkernels and
guest side programs are build via CMake. We provide a script to run all PrIM benchmarks at once for Memclave,
as doing this manually would take too much time. Refer to the section below for usage instructions.

### Example of Building a Single PrIM Benchmark

To build the guest part of the MLP benchmarks, run the following commands in the `ime-client-library` folder:
```bash
cmake -B build .
cmake --build build --target ime-mlp-example
```

The subkernel can be build using the `memclave` docker container as a build environment. Assuming you are
currently cd'd into the `ime` folder, running the following chain of commands will build the MLP subkernel:
```bash
docker run -it --rm -v ./:/ime memclave:latest bash
cd /ime
cmake -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=/usr/share/upmem/cmake/dpu.cmake -B build
cmake --build build --target mlp-example
```

The `mlp-example` subkernel will be located in `./build/mlp-example`. This can now be moved the the guest system.
You will have to rename the subkernel to `mlp` in this case, as paths for the PrIM benchmarks are hardcoded in
some cases. Afterwards, you should be able to run `ime-mlp-example`
from within the build folder, assuming you have moved the `mlp` subkernel to `../build/`.

### Automated Benchmark Execution

We'll start with building the subkernel parts of the PrIM benchmark suite. For this, assuming you are
currently cd'd into the `ime` folder, run the following chain of commands to create the docker build
environment and compile all PrIM subkernels:
```bash
docker run -it --rm -v ./:/ime memclave:latest bash
cd /ime
cmake -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=/usr/share/upmem/cmake/dpu.cmake -B build
cmake --build build --target prim-subkernels
```
After compilation, move the subkernels out of the docker container and on to the memclave environment.
A way to do this, is to first tar the artifacts and then copy out the archive. Assuming your docker
container has id `4fb4dcbfef03`, the following commands will work:
```bash
# start inside the container in /ime

cd build
tar cf prim-subkernels.tar *-example

# run in a seperate terminal
docker cp 4fb4dcbfef03:/ime/build/prim-subkernels.tar ~/Downloads/
```
Then move the subkernels from your Downloads folder, to your memclave environment.
Unpack the into the `ime-client-library` folder in your Memclave environment. If that
folder does not exist yet, copy it over as well.

To compile the client side of the PrIM benchmarks, run the following commands in the
`ime-client-library` folder in the Memclave environment:
```bash
cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -B build .
cmake --build build --target prim-benchmarks
```

There is a slight mismatch between the subkernel names expected by the benchmarks, and
the actual subkernel names. Rename them using the following command:
```bash
for name in $(ls | grep -e '-example'); do mv $name ${name%-example}; done
```

No you should be able to run the PrIM suite for Memclave using the `run.sh` script:
```bash
./run.sh all
```
Once the script has run through, the benchmark results are stored in the file `./build/prim_results.csv`.

<!-- TODO: Must include BFS dataset somehow in the ime-client-library/data folder -->


### PrIM Baseline

We ship the baseline PrIM benchmarks as part of this artifact as well. To get the baseline numbers, you'll
have to move to `prim` folder to your UPMEM execution environment. There you'll have to cd into each of
the benchmarks present in the folder and run `make`. Back in the `prim` folder, you should be able to run the
script `run_prim.py` using python3, which will generate a file called `prim_results.csv`.

*/
